    markdownTitle:
      "# Cresol Evolução promotes education through a plastic bottle cap recycling project.",
    context:
      "One of the oldest organizations in the Cresol System, Cresol Evolução is over 27 years old. Headquartered in the city of Dionísio Cerqueira, in Santa Catarina, the credit union operates in nine other cities in Santa Catarina and is committed to serving its communities with sustainable solutions that generate value beyond credit products. The problems that affect people and the environment can be found in the smallest details. Cresol Evolução noticed that the municipalities in its community had garbage collection, but there was no specific recycling process for plastic bottle caps.\n\nDespite their small size, these objects can have a huge environmental impact. The caps are easily detached from their containers and can contaminate soil and water bodies. The solution to this problem requires, in addition to specific recycling for the materials, collective awareness of the existence of this problem. Cresol Evolução has decided to embrace this mission and generate value with sustainability for its community.",
    development:
      "The Tampinhas Solidárias project involved 19 schools located across the 11 municipalities in western Santa Catarina where the cooperative operates. The initiative reaches a diverse and significant audience: 3,802 students and their families, as well as 408 teachers directly involved in the process. The 10,000 cooperative members are also encouraged to take part in the project, made possible by the work of 140 employees who work to ensure the success of the proposal. The initiative began to take shape in 2024, when the cooperative, through Finep, provided funding to the recycling company Dumi, located in the city of Iporã do Oeste, Santa Catarina. Upon learning about the potential partner’s production process, the Board of Directors encouraged the relationship department to design an initiative that would bring together sustainability, education, and community participation. The project was approved at the beginning of 2025. Each municipality has a reference school for the campaign, and the educational institution is free to involve other local organizations. The caps collected are sold to Dumi, which turns them into polyethylene and donates the proceeds directly to the schools. To boost the results, Cresol Evolução decided to double the financial impact: for every kilogram collected, paid by Dumi at R$2.50/kg, the cooperative adds the same amount through its Social Fund. Engagement is encouraged by the competitive nature of the initiative: at the end of the year, the school that collects the most bottle caps will receive an additional contribution from the Social Fund (ranging from R$1,000 to R$5,000) to be applied to a need chosen by the school community itself. Cresol Evolução branches also act as strategic collection points, encouraging the participation of cooperative members and the community in general. Mobilization also takes place during pre-assemblies, the general assembly, and community events. After the Board’s approval in January 2025 and the selection of schools in February, the collection began the following month and is expected to remain active until November. The first deliveries demonstrate the initiative's potential: in the June collection, 527.7 kg of bottle caps were already collected. In addition to preventing environmental contamination, the project fosters socio-environmental education by encouraging children, youth, and families to reflect on responsible consumption, recycling, and cooperation. The support network — established through collaboration with municipal education departments, school administrators, teachers, and families — helps to strengthen the Tampinhas Solidárias initiative and enhance its results.",
    cooperName:
      "Credit and Savings Cooperative with Solidarity Interaction Cresol Evolution",
    excerpt:
      "Cresol Evolução identified a gap in waste collection in its area of operation, which did not have a specific process for recycling plastic caps. In order to prevent pollution and promote a change in habits, the cooperative worked with schools to promote collection and raise awareness of the issue, as well as providing financial support to educational institutions.",
